summary: A proposed hierarchy_ref type: a node with one name and several parents, no cycle — so a tag can be both a kind of running and a kind of event.

A `tree_ref` gives a node one parent ([[One position per forest]]). That is right for
a file, which has one path, and wrong for a concept that is a kind of two things: a
marathon is a race (`sport/courir`) ''and'' an event (`évènement`). Neither tree
alone says it, and a tag that is "A and B" is not always a conjunction a query could
write — a marathon is more specific than "a race that is an event", and it may have
narrower tags of its own, which are races and events too.

A `hierarchy_ref` is the type for that: a hierarchy where a node may hang under
several parents. `mfr_path` stays a `tree_ref`; the tag vocabulary's `path` becomes a
`hierarchy_ref`. Two types for two meanings, rather than reopening several positions
in a forest, whose problems ([[One position per forest]]) are those of a node with one
path, not of an implication.

Status: decided with the user in October 2026, not built.

!! The value

* ''One name per node'', several parents. A node's rows are one per parent, all bearing the same name; a node with no parent is a root. The same concept cannot be named differently in two places.
* A multi-valued field: adding a parent is an append, leaving one a removal of that row, moving a node a set of its rows.
* `tree_ref`'s JSON shape (`{parent, name}`) per row, under its own `type`.

!! What stays true

* ''No cycle'': a write adding a parent P to a node N is refused (`400`) when N is P or an ancestor of P. Depth ≤ 1000, as for a forest.
* ''Referential integrity'': a parent exists in the same hierarchy; a node with children cannot lose its last row ([[Referential integrity of a forest]]).
* ''Names are unique among the children of each parent'', so a path still names at most one node.

!! Paths

A node has one path per way up to a root: the marathon is `sport/courir/maraton` and
`évènement/maraton`.

* `path = "évènement/maraton"` resolves segment by segment, as in a tree, and finds it.
* `:path`, `osm(path, …)` and `resolve-tree` are multi-valued: a node matches when one of its paths does, and `resolve-tree` returns them all.
* The clients show ''every'' path of a node, not a canonical one.

!! Following

`field ->* …` and `field =>* …` accept a `hierarchy_ref` ([[Following references]]):
the descendants of a node are everything below it by any path, counted once.
`tag -> (path =>* "évènement")` finds the marathons. So subsumption, the tag queries
(`#=évènement`) and the treeref panel get the second parent from the type, with no
convention of their own.

!! In the store

Each node keeps the bitmap of its descendants, as in a forest
([[The forest in the store]]), so a subtree stays one read.

* ''Adding'' an edge N → P adds N and N's descendants to P and to each ancestor of P.
* ''Removing'' an edge cannot subtract: an ancestor may still reach those nodes by another path. The ancestors the edge reached are recomputed bottom-up, each from its children's bitmaps. The cost is the number of those ancestors, not the depth — negligible for a vocabulary of tags, and the reason `mfr_path` does not use this type.

This is the part to test hardest: an oracle recomputing every bitmap from the rows,
compared after random sequences of adds, removals, moves and reverts (the query oracle
already answers `=>*` by walking the rows — [[The query oracle]]).

!! Tags

`mf tag` reads ancestry from the daemon instead of from path strings (today
`is_ancestor("musique", "musique/jazz")` compares strings): the ancestors of a tag are
all its ancestors by every parent. Subsumption and the contradictions keep their
meaning ([[Tag subsumption]]).

Exclusivity was removed first, independently of this type: siblings are ambiguous once
a node has several parents ([[Why tags have no exclusivity]]).

!! Delivery

* A new `Value` variant: a new JSON type, so `API_VERSION` is bumped.
* The tag vocabulary's `path` is converted from `tree_ref` to `hierarchy_ref` — every tree is a valid hierarchy, so the conversion is one-to-one ([[Persisted format changes]], [[Changing a field's type]]).
* [[Why tags are a forest]] and [[One position per forest]] are updated to point here.
